    "narrative": "Heading into the September 7 slate, our optimal lineup is anchored by **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.14) and **Trevor Story** (67.25), who both command top-tier projections due to favorable matchups against mid-rotation arms. **Vinnie Pasquantino** benefits significantly from a high-upside platoon advantage against Law, while **Trevor Story** thrives in a hitter-friendly park environment against Rodriguez. **Seiya Suzuki** also warrants a strong start (63.28); his ability to exploit a platoon advantage against Gasser, coupled with the opposing pitcher's slow delivery\u2014which creates a favorable environment for our priority on stolen bases\u2014makes him a cornerstone of today's scoring strategy. **Griffin Conine** rounds out our high-confidence tier (60.12) as he exploits a favorable matchup against Tong, whose elevated xERA is a primary driver of **Griffin Conine**'s inflated projection.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today\u2019s bench was **Zach McKinstry** (39.94), who narrowly missed the starting cut by mere fractions of a point. Despite a park factor boost, the optimizer penalized **Zach McKinstry** for a challenging matchup against Ryan and a negative platoon split that effectively pushed him below our 40.0 floor. We continue to monitor the status of **Corbin Carroll**, **Josh Naylor**, and **Tyler Soderstrom**, all of whom remain unavailable due to rest or injury, while secondary pieces like **Jose Caballero**, **Mickey Moniak**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Isaac Paredes**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Cole Young**, and **Travis Bazzana** are being held in reserve as we carefully manage our remaining game caps.\n\nRisks for the day center on the left side of our infield. **Austin Riley** (41.94) remains a marginal start facing a high-level arm in Luzardo; the combination of a poor SP matchup and a drop to the #8 spot in the order significantly dampens his outlook. Similarly, **Max Muncy** (43.60) faces an elite challenge in Cease. While we are star